Date: Wed, 27 Jan 93 11:34:15 WST
From: davidc@cs.uwa.edu.au (David Cake)
Subject: Nations as PPLs

> 
> The present international system is an intersting model for what a
> world of PPLs might be like!
> 
> There are 150 or more countries.  Each of them could be considered a
> PPL, each with a particularly complicated contract ("constitution").
> One (effectively) consents to a contract by the fact of being on the
> land owned by a PPL.
> 
The big difference between PPLs and nation states is not so much (IMHO) coercion
or other such moral niceties - it is that nations have a large area of 
geographical land, which is under their control. The only way to change `PPLs`
is by physical movement. 
	Now just imagine retaining the modern system of nations, but with one
crucial difference. Imagine that it was posible, if you held clear title to a
piece of land, to transfer the sovereignty of that land to another nation, and
then (if it was your home) declare yourself a citizen of that country. Then
nations become far more like PPLs than they are now. It is likely that some 
nations undergoing particular economic disaster would then end up with 
virtually no citizens, and could then be 'taken over' by groups of new 
'immigrants', and legally restructured. Is this the back door to PPL?

	Anyone care to comment?
