From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu
Subject: Economic Law (Not Suggestions!)

> True.  But to say that free markets are a natural law in the sense
> that non-free markets have certain negative consequences is much
> different than saying that free markets are inevitable, or saying that
> a group of unorganized humans automatically start interacting in a
> free-market manner.

  This is the nature of spontaneous order. Imagine a group of humans
each with unique resources dropped on an island. The probability
that the resources have been distributed in the optimal manner is very
low. Naturally some of those humans desire the resources of some of the others.
There are two natural ways the system alters itself. Either someone gets
a big stick, clubs a guy over the head and takes his possessions, or they 
decide to trade.  (the other option, called "sharing" doesn't seem to work
except in small families, and even then, conflicts continually occur).

  Is there any doubt that war and trade isn't natural law?

